  node [
    id 324
    label "2GDeqfSK80c"
    step 0
    date "2021-04-08T23:20:20Z"
    title "Analysis of Derek Chauvin Trial (Through Opening Statements)"
    description "Whether on Court TV, the Law &#38; Crime Network, or locally in San Diego on 10News, San Diego criminal defense attorney David P. Shapiro has been called upon to provide legal commentary on what's been happening, and what we can expect to happen, in the criminal trial of the former Minneapolis police officer for the death of George Floyd.  David outlines the first three weeks of the trial so far through opening statements on March 29 in this video.&#10;&#10;Office Address:&#10;Law Office of David P. Shapiro&#10;3500 5th Avenue&#10;Suite 304&#10;San Diego, CA 92103&#10;619-295-3555&#10;https://www.davidpshapirolaw.com/&#10;&#10;Hi, my name is David P Shapiro. I'm the owner and managing partner of the Law Office of David P. Shapiro located in San Diego, California, where my firm helps good people regain control of their future, when charged with a crime. In this video, I want to talk to you about the first couple weeks of what's going on in Minneapolis, the George Floyd trial. Opening statements started on Monday, March 29th, and a lot of times people who are not too familiar with the criminal justice system will be confused about, &#34;Well, what's an opening statement?&#34; For the most part, opening statements are a map where the attorneys will map out to the jury, 12 jurors who will be making the decision of guilt or not guilt for Derek Chauvin, the attorneys will map out what they believe the evidence will show.&#10;&#10;So, they may tell it in a story, imagining if you were there. They may tell it through the perspective of George Floyd. They defense attorney may tell it through the perspective of former Minneapolis police officer Derek Chauvin. And it gives the jury an idea of what is to come. Now, we have heard a lot and understandably and rightfully so in pretty much every instance, of outrage. Outrage about, &#34;Well, what's really the issue here&#34;. There's this video of 8 minutes, 46 seconds, 9 minutes, whatever it is, too long of Chauvin's knee on George Floyd's back, George Floyd's neck, and you literally watched the man die, okay?&#10;&#10;What is important to understand, and this is very, very difficult. And that's why jury selection's so important in any criminal case, particularly in a case like this is because, what a jury will need to do and what a jury has been asked to do is separate everything outside the courtroom and make their decision whether the charges, whether second degree, third degree murder, or second degree manslaughter, have been proven beyond a reasonable doubt, based only on what's presented to them. So we hear a lot. We heard Benjamin Crump repeatedly say, &#34;Why is this a hard case? Look at the video.&#34; And from a social justice perspective, without question. From a police reform perspective, without question. It is an open and shut case, things need to change. Things should have changed 100 years ago, let alone a year ago.&#10;&#10;But the evidence should control this case. And when everything's flushed out, I believe that the prosecution may have a difficult time convicting Derek Chauvin of second degree murder. They may get him on third degree murder, I believe they should, and will get him on manslaughter, at the very least. But it's important to differentiate, as we start up this George Floyd trial, to understand that what we may see on social media, at a press conference, what we may really believe and know what happened, it's ultimately up to the jury. And the hope is that they get it right based on the evidence before them and applying it to the law.&#10;&#10;And then the real impact though of this case will go far beyond what the jury of the 12 people in Minneapolis decide. It will be what we do with it as a society. What we do with it to initiate police reform. What we do with it to maintain and improve on police reform. So when there are signs like officers Derek Chauvin that they're in the pipeline, law enforcement proactively eradicates them, because cops like him give the good cops out there a bad name. Cops like Chauvin fuel this fire in our society, and it's long overdue that we get a handle on it, and we eradicate these officers from the force, and that we enable law enforcement to be proactive in weeding them out.&#10;&#10;And that will hopefully save lives in the future, particularly lives of black men, particularly lives of brown men, particularly the lives of any minority or anyone, any life, we want to save as many lives as possible, and police reform is one way to get there. So that's my take on the first jury selection through opening statements on the George Floyd homicide trial, the trial of defendant, Derek Chauvin, in Minneapolis. Keep an eye out to our YouTube channel. You can follow me on Twitter, @dpshapiro for my take on appearances that I've made discussing the Derek Chauvin trial, as well as my thoughts on the state of the evidence, how I think the case is playing out. And if you have any questions about your matter or have an opportunity where you want to discuss this matter further, we can always be reached at (619) 295-3555."
    channeltitle "Law Office of David P. Shapiro"
    channelid "UCqKSgbTRTPnUlZw7HxoKBTw"
